#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096390 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19a857ac-d36b-5753-a532-4f2a75612d85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "a30b523c-d689-520a-a708-f3b5190eaf9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-477" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-\\sin^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\cos^2 x = 1-\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{1 - \\sin^{2} x}dx &= ∫\\frac{1}{\\cos^{2} x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫(\\frac{1}{\\cos x})^{2}dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\sec^{2} xdx \\\\\n&= \\tan x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096391 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9934a8da-2378-5019-a3a3-7cd66cbeeaa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "872b9d99-ea39-540f-af2e-acbfa28186b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-478" :question/problem "Calculate $\\, \\displaystyle{\\int} \\tan^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a $\\tan^2{x}$ term. So we can use the identity\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2x =\\sec^2x\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x =\\sec^2 x -1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\tan^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\tan x -x+C. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096392 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0ba6d15-a20e-5f16-b324-0bbead5458ca" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Cosecant-Cotangent Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "d7c09fca-2f4b-5a0a-b687-938527b44d62" :question/math-academy-id "e-479" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\left(5\\cot^2 x -x^2\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+\\cot^2x =\\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nRearranging this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\cot^2 x &=\\csc^2 x -1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\left(5\\cot^2 x -x^2\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\bigg(5\\left(\\csc^2 x -1 \\right) -x^2\\bigg) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(5\\csc^2 x -5 -x^2\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &=-5\\cot x -5x-\\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096393 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5822b4-c5d4-59a8-bd72-a41d3074e4e6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Expressions With Linear Arguments Using the Pythagorean Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "14ab2251-66fc-5f94-9821-f14fcc2c03e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-480" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} 2\\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d} x$." :question/worked-solution "From the identity\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 \\theta =\\sec^2 \\theta\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\theta = 3x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 1+\\tan^2 (3x) &=\\sec^2 (3x)\\ \\tan^2 (3x) &=\\sec^2 (3x) -1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int 2\\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x&= 2\\int \\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &= 2\\int \\left(\\sec^2 (3x) -1\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &= 2\\left(\\int \\sec^2 (3x)\\, \\textrm{d}x -\\int1 \\, \\textrm{d}x\\right) \\\\ &= 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\tan (3x) -x\\right)+C \\\\ &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\tan (3x) -2x+C. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9934a8da-2378-5019-a3a3-7cd66cbeeaa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d44866d-ea9f-5eb1-b3